{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/z21ojipo","slideIndex":2,"position":{"left":742,"top":270,"width":410,"height":150},"name":"right-panel-main","text":"May a Bar Association refuse representation to a named accused or category of accused?","geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":27,"typeface":"Aptos","color":"#111111","bold":true,"alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/36h4re5g","slideIndex":2,"position":{"left":742,"top":456,"width":410,"height":72},"name":"right-panel-context","text":"The Court considered Article 22(1) and the Bar Council of India Rules on professional conduct.","geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":18,"typeface":"Aptos","color":"#333333","alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"notes","id":"nt/lqujws","slideIndex":2,"text":"The Supreme Court recorded the resolution as one refusing representation to accused policemen. It then placed that incident in the wider context of similar resolutions against persons accused of heinous crimes or other unpopular accused. The core issue is the legality and ethical propriety of a collective boycott. Source: judgment, paras 14–16 and 30–32, https://indiankanoon.org/doc/153132263/."}
