{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/v6hs3alg","slideIndex":3,"position":{"left":172,"top":176,"width":420,"height":44},"name":"anchor1-title","text":"Article 22(1)","geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":31,"typeface":"Aptos","color":"#111111","bold":true,"alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/u58ru54v","slideIndex":3,"position":{"left":172,"top":230,"width":420,"height":118},"name":"anchor1-text","text":"An arrested person must not be denied the right to consult and be defended by a legal practitioner of their choice.","geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":23,"typeface":"Aptos","color":"#333333","alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"shape","id":"sh/g3yps3yl","slideIndex":3,"position":{"left":640,"top":174,"width":2,"height":390},"name":"middle-line","geometry":"rect","fillColor":"#D9D9D9","lineWidth":0}
{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/h476loz6","slideIndex":3,"position":{"left":700,"top":176,"width":86,"height":46},"name":"anchor2-num","text":"02","geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":26,"typeface":"Aptos","color":"#555555","bold":true,"alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/u1g7qtgf","slideIndex":3,"position":{"left":800,"top":176,"width":400,"height":44},"name":"anchor2-title","text":"BCI Rules, Chapter II","geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":31,"typeface":"Aptos","color":"#111111","bold":true,"alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/v2pojyh0","slideIndex":3,"position":{"left":800,"top":230,"width":400,"height":142},"name":"anchor2-text","text":"An advocate is generally bound to accept a brief at a fee consistent with their standing and the case, subject to special circumstances.","geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":23,"typeface":"Aptos","color":"#333333","alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"shape","id":"sh/8zypojy9","slideIndex":3,"position":{"left":72,"top":430,"width":1136,"height":5},"name":"bottom-rule","geometry":"rect","fillColor":"#222222","lineWidth":0}
{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/t076hozu","slideIndex":3,"position":{"left":72,"top":468,"width":1136,"height":76},"name":"bottom-statement","text":"Individual choice may exist; collective coercion cannot extinguish access to counsel.","geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":33,"typeface":"Aptos","color":"#222222","bold":true,"alignment":"center"}}
{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/mdg7m9gj","slideIndex":3,"position":{"left":132,"top":558,"width":1016,"height":38},"name":"bottom-note","text":"The judgment ties professional ethics to the rule of law and a fair criminal process.","geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":20,"typeface":"Aptos","color":"#333333","alignment":"center"}}
{"kind":"notes","id":"nt/4uc4eo","slideIndex":3,"text":"The Court quoted Article 22(1) of the Constitution and Chapter II of the Bar Council of India Rules. The Rule says an advocate is bound to accept a brief at a fee consistent with standing and the nature of the case, while special circumstances may justify refusal. The holding invalidated association-wide resolutions; it does not mean every lawyer must accept every brief in all circumstances. Source: judgment, paras 30–32, https://indiankanoon.org/doc/153132263/."}
